<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Partial Variation 3 - Jersey printing</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The cost of producing school jerseys, \(C\), is partly a fixed setup cost and partly varies with the number of jerseys produced, \(n\): \(C = kn + b\).</p>
<p>Producing \({@pn1@}\) jerseys costs \({@pC1@}\) Ksh. Producing \({@pn2@}\) jerseys costs \({@pC2@}\) Ksh.</p>
<p>(a) Find the cost per jersey, \(k\).</p>
<p>\(k = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>(b) Find the fixed setup cost, \(b\).</p>
<p>\(b = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>

<p><strong>(a) Find the cost per jersey \(k\)</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step 1: write an equation for each order.</strong> Substitute each pair of values into \(C = kn + b\):</p>
\[ {@pC1@} = {@pn1@}k + b \qquad \text{(1)} \]
\[ {@pC2@} = {@pn2@}k + b \qquad \text{(2)} \]
<p><strong>Step 2: subtract equation (1) from equation (2).</strong> Both equations contain \(+\,b\), so subtracting them removes \(b\) and leaves only \(k\):</p>
\[ {@pC2@} - {@pC1@} = {@pn2@}k - {@pn1@}k \quad\Longrightarrow\quad {@pC2-pC1@} = {@pn2-pn1@}k. \]
<p>The extra \({@pn2-pn1@}\) jerseys cost an extra Ksh \({@pC2-pC1@}\). The setup cost is paid once whatever the size of the order, so it isn't part of this difference.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: divide both sides by \({@pn2-pn1@}\).</strong> \(k\) is multiplied by \({@pn2-pn1@}\), so dividing by \({@pn2-pn1@}\) leaves \(k\) on its own:</p>
\[ k = \frac{ {@pC2-pC1@} }{ {@pn2-pn1@} } = {@pk@}. \]
<p>So each jersey costs Ksh \({@pk@}\) to produce.</p>

<p><strong>(b) Find the fixed setup cost \(b\)</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step 1: substitute \(k = {@pk@}\) into equation (1).</strong></p>
\[ {@pC1@} = {@pk@} \times {@pn1@} + b. \]
<p><strong>Step 2: multiply.</strong> \({@pk@} \times {@pn1@} = {@pk*pn1@}\), so</p>
\[ {@pC1@} = {@pk*pn1@} + b. \]
<p><strong>Step 3: subtract \({@pk*pn1@}\) from both sides.</strong></p>
\[ b = {@pC1@} - {@pk*pn1@} = {@pb@}. \]
<p>So the fixed setup cost is Ksh \({@pb@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
